#################### Step 1: User Starts SCP #################### 

On PC1:
scp file.txt user@192.168.2.20:/home/user/

At this point:

SCP starts.
SCP asks SSH to establish a secure connection.
SSH requests a TCP connection to PC2 on TCP Port 22.
No packets have been sent yet.
#################### Step 2: TCP Creates the SYN #################### 

TCP creates the first packet of the three-way handshake.

Example:

Source Port      : 52341
Destination Port : 22
Sequence Number  : 1000
ACK Number       : 0
Flags            : SYN
Window Size      : 65535
Checksum

At this stage TCP only creates the segment.

TCP does NOT know:

MAC Address
Gateway
Router
Switch

It simply creates the TCP segment and passes it to the IP layer.

#################### Step 3: IP Layer Adds the IP Header #################### 

The Network Layer encapsulates the TCP segment inside an IP packet.

Source IP      : 192.168.1.10
Destination IP : 192.168.2.20
TTL            : 64
Protocol        : TCP

Now the IP layer asks:

Is 192.168.2.20 inside my subnet?

My Network

192.168.1.10/24

Destination

192.168.2.20/24

Answer:

NO

Therefore IP decides:

I cannot send directly to PC2.

I must send this packet to my Default Gateway.

192.168.1.1
#################### Step 4: ARP for the Gateway #################### 

PC1 knows:

Gateway IP

192.168.1.1

But it does not know the MAC address.

It sends an ARP Request.

Who has 192.168.1.1 ?

Tell 192.168.1.10

Destination MAC

FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF

(Broadcast)

The Switch receives the broadcast.

Because ARP is a broadcast,

the switch floods it to every port in VLAN 1.

The Router replies:

192.168.1.1 is

AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA

PC1 stores it inside the ARP Cache.

